.TH MODBUS-FAKE-INVERTER 1 "2026-11-05" "pv-suite 0.5.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
modbus-fake-inverter \- simulated PV inverter for testing the pv-suite
stack
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B modbus-fake-inverter
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B modbus-fake-inverter \-\-static
.RI [ options ]

.SH DESCRIPTION
A stdlib-only Modbus TCP server that plays an inverter \(em with a
battery, a smart meter, the modbus-cache diagnostics block and the
pv-suite's register-map profiles \(em so the scraper, the cache and the
whole stack can be exercised without hardware. The shipped default
profile is the sun2000 (Huawei SUN2000) map; Deye hybrids are included.
This is a test instrument:
synthetic data only, no state worth protecting beyond its LAN segment.
The startup surface is the family's: the flags, the bare env twins and
the set-grammar config file (\fB--config\fR, every line a
`set <name> <value>` \(em the bundled sample is
\fBinverter/examples/modbus-fake-inverter.conf\fR) \(em flag > env > config >
default.
.PP
It simulates a sunny day: a double-shouldered PV curve over
two MPPT strings (east/west), a home load with morning/evening peaks, a
battery state machine (95 % charge efficiency, self-discharge, an 80 %
discharge cap, temperature derating), per-phase grid voltages and load
shares (mean-reverting wanderers), a grid-frequency machine, and a grid
outage machine (random blackouts). All machines integrate with a simulated
clock \(em UTC unless told otherwise, freezable (\fB--time\fR) and scalable
(\fB--time-scale\fR for time-lapse). \fB--static\fR replaces the day with
constant demo values. The profile's per-entry \fBfc\fR picks each
entry's address space (FC3 holding / FC4 input registers, FC1 coils /
FC2 discrete inputs for \fBbit\fR entries); writes land in overlays
the scrapers read (FC06/FC16 the registers, FC05/FC15 the coils, plus
\fBset coil\fR/\fBset discrete\fR on the control socket);
\fB--chaos\fR answers a fraction of requests hostile.
.PP
Modbus auth (\fB--modbus-auth\fR): with \fBsuite\fR the fake's clients prove
a shared key (\fB--modbus-auth-key\fR, min 16 characters) with a crypt
handshake right after every connect \(em 52 bytes: magic, fresh nonce,
HMAC\-SHA256 proof; the fake answers \fBOK\fR (plain Modbus follows) or
\fBERR\fR (the client is dropped before it ever sees a register).
Empty auth = no auth at all \(em the trusted-LAN posture
stands. The writes are gated like the cache's (\fB--allow-write\fR):
off = every Modbus write (FC05/06/15/16) is refused with
exception 01 and audited WRITE\-REFUSED; on = a write lands in the
overlay, audited WRITE.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-listen " listen"
listen address HOST:PORT
.TP
.BI \-\-unit " unit"
Modbus unit id served
.TP
.BI \-\-modbus\-profile " modbus-profile"
device profile JSON (the shared profiles/ schema)
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-kwp " max-kwp"
PV array size in kWp, peak = 75%% of it
.TP
.BI \-\-timezone " timezone"
clock the state machines integrate over (the PV bell, the home load, the
clouds, the battery, the grid voltage): UTC or the local wall clock
.TP
.BI \-\-time " time"
freeze simulated time HH:MM
.TP
.BI \-\-time\-scale " time-scale"
the simulated clock speed: N times real time \- every machine (the
battery, the clouds, the voltages, the day counters) integrates over it,
so a full simulated day takes 24/N real hours
.TP
.B \-\-static
constant demo values instead of the day simulation
.TP
.BI \-\-battery\-kwh " battery-kwh"
usable battery capacity in kWh
.TP
.B \-\-strict
reject reads of undefined registers with exception 02 (like the real
device)
.TP
.BI \-\-chaos " chaos"
probability 0..1 that a request is answered hostile and dropped; seed
with \-\-seed for reproducibility
.TP
.BI \-\-grid\-voltage " grid-voltage"
nominal grid phase voltage in V (120 or 230; the machine wanders
±grid\-voltage\-wander around it, the grid\-voltage setpoint overrides)
.TP
.BI \-\-grid\-voltage\-wander " grid-voltage-wander"
the per\-phase voltage machine's wander range as a fraction of the
nominal (0.1 = the phases wander ±10 %%; 0 = the flat grid); the live
set re\-clamps the walk
.TP
.BI \-\-grid\-frequency " grid-frequency"
nominal grid frequency in Hz (the machine wanders
±grid\-frequency\-wander around it, the grid\-frequency setpoint
overrides)
.TP
.BI \-\-grid\-frequency\-wander " grid-frequency-wander"
the frequency machine's clamp in Hz around the nominal (0.2 = ±0.2 Hz; 0
= the flat frequency); the live set re\-clamps the walk
.TP
.BI \-\-seed " seed"
chaos RNG seed
.TP
.BI \-\-modbus\-auth " modbus-auth"
the Modbus auth scheme: `suite` demands the crypt handshake (magic +
nonce + HMAC proof of the shared key) right after every connect — the
peer answers OK, or ERR and the session drops; none/empty = no auth at
all
.TP
.BI \-\-modbus\-auth\-key " modbus-auth-key"
the shared key the fake's clients prove with the suite handshake (min 16
characters); the value may be `file:<path>` — the first line of an
owner\-only 0600 file (a systemd LoadCredential rides as
file:/run/credentials/...)
.TP
.BI \-\-connect\-acl " connect-acl"
the Modbus clients allowed to connect (the cache's connect\-acl,
mirrored): "any" (the trusted\-LAN posture), "none", or a
comma\-separated IP/CIDR list
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-write
accept Modbus writes (FC05/06/15/16) from the fake's clients: a write
lands in the write overlay, logged and audited WRITE like the cache's
forwarded ones; off = every write is refused with exception 01 and
audited WRITE\-REFUSED — the cache's \-\-allow\-write gate, mirrored
(env ALLOW_WRITE)
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-clients " max-clients"
refuse further connections past this many simultaneous clients, 0 = no
limit
.TP
.BI \-\-client\-timeout " client-timeout"
disconnect clients silent for this many seconds, 0 = never
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-conn\-per\-ip " max-conn-per-ip"
refuse further connections from one peer IP past this many simultaneous
connections, 0 = no per\-IP limit
.TP
.BI \-\-control\-port " control-port"
JSON\-lines control server target: unix socket path or TCP port number
on 127.0.0.1; 0 = off
.TP
.BI \-\-control\-token " control-token"
require a token on control connections: the client authenticates with
{"cmd": "auth", "token":...}; over the unix socket a trusted peer (root,
the daemon's own user or \-\-control\-trust\-uids — SO_PEERCRED) skips
the token; the value may be `file:<path>` — the first line of an
owner\-only 0600 file (a systemd LoadCredential rides as
file:/run/credentials/...)
.TP
.BI \-\-control\-ops\-token " control-ops-token"
a second, read\-only control token: sessions authenticating with it may
run its policy's verbs only (the default policy: show) (no set/clear, no
reset, no shutdown) — the ops shift against the admin key, both audited;
the value may be `file:<path>` — the first line of an owner\-only 0600
file (a systemd LoadCredential rides as file:/run/credentials/...)
.TP
.BI \-\-control\-ops\-verbs " control-ops-verbs"
the read\-only ops role's RBAC policy: the verbs its sessions may run,
comma\-separated
.TP
.BI \-\-control\-trust\-uids " control-trust-uids"
extra peer uids the unix control socket trusts without the token
(SO_PEERCRED — a local systemd unit speaks without the secret); root and
the daemon's own user are always trusted
.TP
.B \-\-control\-socket\-group
leave the unix control socket group\-accessible (chmod 0660 instead of
the plain 0600): a local group may speak to the control wire without the
token ($CONTROL_SOCKET_GROUP)
.TP
.B \-\-log\-access
log client connects/disconnects at INFO instead of DEBUG
.TP
.B \-\-local\-time
log timestamps in the system time zone instead of UTC
.TP
.B \-\-control\-log\-replies
DEBUG\-log the full output of every control command
.TP
.B \-\-metrics\-server
serve the tool's own Prometheus /metrics endpoint (the live `set metrics
enable` toggle exists too)
.TP
.BI \-\-metrics\-listen " metrics-listen"
the metrics bind address HOST:PORT — 0.0.0.0:PORT serves every
interface, a token is mandatory beyond loopback (a token\-less
non\-loopback bind refuses to start, F13)
.TP
.BI \-\-metrics\-token " metrics-token"
require Authorization: Bearer <token> on the /metrics endpoint (the same
scheme the scraper speaks); the value may be `file:<path>` — the first
line of an owner\-only 0600 file (a systemd LoadCredential rides as
file:/run/credentials/...)
.TP
.BI \-\-metrics\-acl " metrics-acl"
clients allowed to connect to the /metrics endpoint: "any", "none"
(refuse everyone), comma\-separated IPs/prefixes like
10.0.0.7,10.48.0.0/16, or "file:<path>" — the standalone ip\-prefix acl
file (the gate object {"type": "ip\-prefix", "rules": [{"type":
"allow"|"deny", "prefix": ...}]}: the last matching rule decides, a
client no rule decides refuses; "any" = 0.0.0.0/0, the whole space);
checked at accept — a client off the ACL is closed without a reply
.TP
.BI \-\-metrics\-max\-clients " metrics-max-clients"
maximum concurrent HTTP clients on the /metrics endpoint; extras get an
immediate 503, 0 = unlimited
.TP
.BI \-\-metrics\-client\-timeout " metrics-client-timeout"
seconds an idle /metrics client is kept before the socket times out, 0 =
the built\-in 5 s cap
.TP
.BI \-\-metrics\-max\-conn\-per\-ip " metrics-max-conn-per-ip"
maximum concurrent /metrics connections from one peer IP, 0 = no per\-IP
limit
.TP
.BI \-\-audit\-log " audit-log"
the audit trail: a file path or \- for stdout (the docker/journald way);
`none` turns the local file off (with a forward spelled that is the
remote\-only mode); the XDG state home when unset
.TP
.BI \-\-audit\-forward " audit-forward"
also forward the audit trail: syslog (the local socket auto\-scan),
syslog:/path (an explicit socket), udp://host[:514] (RFC5426,
best\-effort) or tcp://host[:514] (RFC6587 framing) — the off\-box copy
carries the per\-writer hash chain, so a scheduled search at the
collector can alert on a chain break; docs/AUDIT.md
.TP
.BI \-\-audit\-forward\-format " audit-forward-format"
the remote forward's envelope: RFC5424 (the default) or CEF — the local
syslog forms keep the verbatim send (remote targets only)
.TP
.BI \-\-audit\-instance " audit-instance"
the audit lines' instance=<id> word — which daemon wrote the line: the
control port when unset/empty (unique per host by construction), the
override when spelled; 1\-64 characters (letters, digits, . _ : @ \-);
make an override unique among everything that shares one collector
.TP
.BI \-\-audit\-max\-mb " audit-max-mb"
rotate the audit log at this size in MB, keeping N backups; 0 = no size
rotation
.TP
.B \-\-audit\-daily
also rotate the audit log at UTC midnight
.TP
.BI \-\-audit\-files " audit-files"
rotated audit backups to keep
.TP
.B \-\-no\-audit\-log
disable the audit trail
.TP
.BI \-\-log\-level " log-level"
the log verbosity: DEBUG adds client traffic and per\-poll detail
.TP
.BI \-\-dump\-path " dump-path"
the state dump: the live setting overrides (the tokens apart) as one
JSON, written 0600 with an integrity hash (a restore refuses a tampered,
unsigned or group\-writable dump); dumped on the request dump command,
the graceful shutdown and every \-\-dump\-interval seconds; loaded on
start only with \-\-restore\-on\-start, on demand with the request
restore command
.TP
.BI \-\-dump\-key " dump-key"
the key that seals the state dumps and the reload's state file: the
state rides encrypted (an HMAC\-SHA256 counter\-mode keystream,
authenticated — a restore refuses a wrong or missing key, and a
configured key refuses an unsealed dump); the reload re\-exec
re\-resolves the same key, so the state file reads on the other side; no
key = the dumps stay 0600 plaintext with the integrity hash; a dump path
without a key warns at start and on set; the value may be `file:<path>`
— the first line of an owner\-only 0600 file (a systemd LoadCredential
rides as file:/run/credentials/...)
.TP
.BI \-\-dump\-interval " dump-interval"
seconds between the automatic state dumps, 0 = on request (the request
dump command and the shutdown)
.TP
.B \-\-restore\-on\-start
load the state dump when the daemon starts (needs \-\-dump\-path) — a
restart serves last\-known values instead of cold misses; without it the
dump loads on demand
.TP
.B \-\-config
config file — the set grammar: every line a `set <name> <value>`,
comments start with # ($CONFIG, then modbus\-fake\-inverter.conf next to
this script, then the subproject root)
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
validate the configuration, print the effective settings and exit
(nothing is bound or connected)


.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGHUP
re-apply the environment layer (all-or-nothing).
.TP
.B SIGUSR1
reset the scenario: runtime pins, the cloud machine, the write overlay
and the pinned coils/discrete inputs
are dropped.
.TP
.BR SIGTERM ", " SIGINT
graceful shutdown with a session summary.

.SH CONTROL
The control server speaks JSON lines
(\fB{"cmd": "..."}\fR -> \fB{"status": "ok|fail|note|bye", "text": "..."}\fR;
TCP requires the token when configured). Commands: \fBset\fR/\fBclear\fR/
\fBshow\fR/\fBhelp\fR (the settings: pv, load, batt, grid, grid-voltage,
grid-frequency, grid-outage, soc, mppt, cloud, timezone, time, time-scale,
scenario, chaos, seed, battery-state, strict, static, max-kwp,
battery-kwh, max-clients, client-timeout, max-conn-per-ip,
metrics-server, metrics-acl, register, coil, discrete,
log-level, modbus auth, modbus profile (the suite
scheme; the key
\(em modbus auth key \(em is a startup-only secret: \fBshow\fR refuses
it, the wire refuses set/clear) \(em and the token lives: control token,
the ops token, metrics token, each startup-only, the value never
rendering \(em \fBshow <secret>\fR refuses),
\fBshow connections\fR (the live Modbus clients: peer,
age, requests), \fBshow registers\fR,
\fBshow version\fR, \fBshow knobs\fR (the dictionary of the registry),
\fBinspect\fR, \fBreset\fR, \fBrequest reload\fR (the whole-daemon restart
that keeps the pins: the overrides save to a state file beside the config
script, the process re-execs, the
fresh fake re-applies them once), \fBrequest reload-config\fR (re-read
the config file without the restart: the config's values re-apply over
the running machines \(em the demo knobs, not the pins), with a dump
path configured
(\fB--dump-path\fR): \fBrequest dump\fR / \fBrequest restore\fR (the
overrides as one 0600 integrity-hashed JSON; \fB--dump-key\fR seals it
\(em the state rides encrypted, a restore refuses a wrong or missing key;
\fB--restore-on-start\fR
loads it at start, \fB--dump-interval\fR rewrites it on a timer),
\fBshutdown\fR,
\fBquit\fR. A second, read-only token (\fB--control-ops-token\fR)
authenticates into the show verbs only \(em the ops shift against the
admin key, both roles audited. The control listener rides the family's
live rebind: \fBset control-port\fR (a unix path, a TCP port on
127.0.0.1, or off) swaps the socket without dropping the connected
sessions \(em tokenless TCP included, the F11 test-tool posture \(em and
the family's control knobs all set live: \fBcontrol ops verbs\fR (the
read-only role's policy), \fBcontrol trust-uids\fR (the peer-cred
list), \fBcontrol socket-group\fR (0660 instead of 0600, the live
socket re-modes). See
.BR modbus-fake-cli (1).

.SH SECURITY
The fake is a development/test instrument, not a security-first design
(the audit's F11): the Modbus surface speaks by default to any peer that
can reach the port \(em no TLS; the opt-in \fB--connect-acl\fR IP gate
(the cache's connect-acl, mirrored: \fBany\fR/\fBnone\fR/the
IP/CIDR list, live-settable) filters who may connect at all, and the
optional \fB--auth suite\fR
shared-key handshake authenticates clients when a demo wants it \(em and
the metrics endpoint
speaks the family's F13 shape: it refuses to start beyond loopback
without a token (the loopback bind serves tokenless), and the
\fB--metrics-acl\fR IP gate narrows who reaches the endpoint at all
(the metrics-acl: \fBany\fR/\fBnone\fR/the IP/prefix list or
\fBfile:<path>\fR, checked at accept \(em a client off the ACL is
closed without a reply). Run it on a trusted,
LAN-isolated network, next to the tools it is meant to serve.
\fB--max-clients\fR/\fB--client-timeout\fR cap runaway connections and
the read-count gate keeps a malformed frame from assembling an oversized
reply. The control surface can still carry the family's read-only ops
token (\fB--control-ops-token\fR) when the ops shift only ever watches;
the unix control socket is 0600, or 0660 with
\fB--control-socket-group\fR (a local group shares it). The audit
trail (prefix INV, the XDG state home when \fB--audit-log\fR is
unset) forwards like the family's: \fB--audit-forward\fR spells the
target \(em \fBsyslog\fR (the local socket auto\-scan),
\fBsyslog:/path\fR, \fBudp://host[:514]\fR or
\fBtcp://host[:514]\fR (the off\-box copy; the remote envelope rides
\fB--audit-forward-format\fR = \fBrfc5424\fR|\fBcef\fR) \(em a
send failure notes once and the next event re\-arms. Every line names
its writer daemon in \fBinstance=<id>\fR \(em the control port when
\fB--audit-instance\fR is unset (unique per host by construction), the
override when spelled (1\-64 characters, letters, digits, . _ : @ \-);
make an override unique among everything that shares one collector: the
chain groups by it;
\fB--audit-log none\fR turns the local file off (with a forward
spelled: the remote\-only mode; without one: the audit disabled).

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CHAOS ", " MAX_KWP ", " PROFILE ,
.B STRICT ", " TIME ", " TIME_SCALE ", " LOG_LEVEL
the demo layer: the start reads it too (flag > env > the config script)
and SIGHUP re-applies it (keep-last-good).
.TP
.B CTL
the control target when \fB--control-port\fR is unset.
.TP
.B LISTEN ", " MAX_CLIENTS ", " CONTROL_TOKEN ", " ...
every flag has an environment twin of the same bare name (no tool
prefix); the flag wins over the env, the env over the config script.
.TP
.B CONTROL_TOKEN ", " CLI_KEEPALIVE
the clients' TCP token and idle-ping interval.
.TP
.B CONFIG
the config script when \fB--config\fR is unset.

.SH FILES
.TP
.I modbus-fake-inverter.conf
the config script (the set grammar) \(em next to the binary when
\fB--config\fR is unset (the image ships it at
/pv\-suite/inverter/modbus\-fake\-inverter.conf).
.TP
.I /tmp/fake-inverter-<port>.socket
the control socket used when \fB--control-port\fR is unset.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/fake-inverter/audit.log
the audit trail when \fB--audit-log\fR is unset.

.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
success (\fB--dry-run\fR: valid configuration).
.TP
.B 1
invalid configuration or a failed bind (reason on stderr).
.TP
.B 2
command-line misuse.

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR modbus-fake-cli (1),
.BR modbus-cache (1),
.BR modbus-scraper (1)
.PP
\fBinverter/README.md\fR in the suite checkout \(em the full documentation,
including the state machines.
